{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "6",
    "case_summary": "A 68-year-old man with pseudophakic bullous keratopathy underwent Descemet Stripping Automated Endothelial Keratoplasty (DSAEK) in the right eye. His postoperative course was initially uneventful under topical and systemic corticosteroids. He had a history of an eye infection by Thaumetopoea pityocampa (pine processionary) 9 months before DSAEK, which had resolved prior to surgery.\n\nTwo months after DSAEK, he was referred for evaluation of keratitis in the right eye. On examination:\n• Best-corrected visual acuity was 20/200 in the right eye and 20/20 in the left eye.  \n• Pupils were isocoric and isoreactive without an afferent pupillary defect.  \n• There was no pain with ocular movements.  \n• Slit-lamp biomicroscopy of the right eye showed brown, multilobular lesions confined to the deep corneal stroma, with no conjunctival hyperemia, no discharge, and no epithelial defect.  \n• The anterior chamber was deep and clear. Intraocular pressure and fundus examination were normal. The left eye was unremarkable.\n\nAnterior segment optical coherence tomography of the right eye revealed a central infiltrate involving all corneal layers and a peripheral infiltrate concentrated in the deep stroma.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Unilateral Corneal Infiltrate with Deep Stromal Involvement",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.6,
